{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1570841251,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0MTI2NTQxMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3767#issuecomment-541265412"},"message":"First, the more general issue of conflicts.  We certainly should extend rerun with an interface to let the users handle those (i.e. the longstanding gh-2092).  This is even more important now that support for rerunning merges has landed in master.\n\n\u003e So cherry-picking fails, also semantically the action is clear -- remove the files, and ideally `rerun` should do that on our behalf. What do you think @kyleam ?\n\nNo, I'm not convinced rerun should try to be clever here and attempt to automatically resolve conflicts for the user.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1570847780,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0MTI3NTcxNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3767#issuecomment-541275715"},"message":"\u003e No, I'm not convinced rerun should try to be clever here and attempt to automatically resolve conflicts for the user.\n\nDo you see **any** use case when upon rerun, cherry picking of removal of a file (annexed or not) would require user consideration so he would make a decision to keep that file?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1571087485,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0MTkyNDIwMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3767#issuecomment-541924201"},"message":"\u003e Do you see **any** use case when upon rerun, cherry picking of removal of a file (annexed or not) would require user consideration so he would make a decision to keep that file?\n\nHere's a case that doesn't seem too implausible to me.  A user runs a command with some non-deterministic output\n\n```\ncommit d55651eea0679ad08d886034927bfeca4881dcdc\n\n    [DATALAD RUNCMD] calculate y for each x\n\n    \u003csome operation with randomness and no seed\u003e\n```\n\nthen has a follow up commit like this:\n\n```\ncommit 4d09a59ec6c1d487824a36ac56504b9130c742c2 (HEAD -\u003e master)\n\n    Drop y files for a few x's\n\n    The next step will read in all data files in the y/ subdirectory.\n    Most of these files look OK, but for a few x's there are NA values.\n    I'll have to look into what's going on there, but I really need\n    preliminary results of the other y's now.  Delete the NA-containing\n    files for now.\n```\n\nOn a rerun, those files might not have the NA values, and as a user I would certainly want to have this conflict presented to me.\n\nThat last scenario is assuming a _complete_ rerun of the history.  But `rerun` also supports moving onto an arbitrary new base.  In that case, you don't even need non-deterministic output to run into problematic conflicts.\n\nBut the particular case doesn't matter too much.  I very much doubt my ability to foresee even most of the possible scenarios, and I would have to be comfortable doing that before overriding git's decision that this is a conflict that should be presented to the user.  The user has decided to remove a file with specific content.  If a cherry pick tries to remove a file with different content, there is new information.  It is the user that can decide what to do with this new information and whether a deletion is still the action they want.  The content could mean or be anything and the removal could be for any reason (only the user knows).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1571516529,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU0NDE5NDM5Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3767#issuecomment-544194396"},"message":"A nice example @kyleam !  Indeed, shouldn't be done \"by default\".\n\nSo we need to provide an option/convenience to be able to instruct `rerun` what to do in case of such cherry-picking problems. \n\nOne way would indeed be `interactive`.  May be #2092 could be a bit \"flipped inside out\" -- instead of `rerun` to somehow record its stage, and fail with instructions on what to do so it could pick up where it left of (similar to what `git` does), `rerun` could just open a new interactive shell (tty), with instructions to user to resolve all conflicts and `git add/rm` what needed, and just quit (Ctrl-d, or \"exit\") that shell.  Then `rerun` would continue with commit/save'ing (may be adding some note into the commit message on what conflicted) and proceed forward.\n\nBut I also see value in options for non-interactive resolution, such as in this case an option to `redo-removes` (or alike), which would in case of a \"conflict\" just `git rm -f` those files on users' behalf and continue.","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1579431954,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI5NjA4MTcwNTc="},"added":["severity-minor"],"removed":[]}]}